# Bytecode caches. The test suite disables these itself (sys.dont_write_bytecode),
# so this is belt and braces for ad-hoc python3 invocations.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The research index is built by tools/ingest.py and is not source. It can be
# large, and it is a cache of other people's pages, so it should never be
# committed. Re-create it with:  tools/ingest.py --url <page>
state/research/index.db
state/research/index.db-*

# state/audit.log IS tracked: it is the record of what ran, and the project
# treats it as the trustworthy source over AGENT_LAST_SCORE in any agent.conf.
# Everything else under state/ is generated and disposable.
state/profile/

# ...which includes these two, and the comment above was claiming it before the
# rules did. Both are rewritten by every ./bootstrap.sh detect, so committing them
# means a spurious diff on every hardware change AND - worse - it presents one
# machine's profile as the project's configuration. profile.env on this box
# records an i5-5300U with no GPU and AI_TIER="cpu", which is the single knob
# every model decision keys off. On the A5000 it would be wrong immediately.
state/detect-history.log
state/previous-tier

# config/models.env and config/profile.env are the same category one level up,
# and are excluded for the same reason plus one more: they are both stamped
# GENERATED - do not edit by hand, and rule 2 already says never edit them.
# Their absence is not silent breakage. A fresh clone reports one warning per
# agent saying "run ./bootstrap.sh detect" and exits non-zero, so the first
# command a new machine runs tells it what to do. Verified by deleting both from
# a copy of the tree: 16 actionable warnings, then 359 assertions and the one
# known error after detect. A missing file that names its own fix is better
# than a committed file that is confidently wrong on every other machine.
config/models.env
config/profile.env

# config/hardware.json is NOT excluded, and the distinction is worth keeping:
# it is hand-authored and records the card being planned FOR, not the card this
# machine happens to have. Its "release" field does leak the local kernel
# string, which is harmless and not worth losing the file over.
